## THEORY

Kubernetes is an open-source container orchestration platform used to deploy, manage, and scale containerized applications.

Minikube:
Minikube creates a local Kubernetes cluster for development and testing.

Hyper-V:
Hyper-V is a virtualization technology that allows virtual machines to run on Windows.

kubectl:
kubectl is a command-line tool used to communicate with and manage Kubernetes clusters.

Pod:
A pod is the smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes. It contains one or more containers.

Deployment:
A Deployment manages application pods and maintains the desired application state.

Environment Variables:
Environment variables provide configuration values to containers without requiring changes to application code.

Service:
A Service provides a stable way to access a group of pods.

NodePort:
NodePort exposes a Kubernetes service through a port on the node, allowing access from outside the cluster when networking permits.

Kubernetes Dashboard:
The dashboard provides a graphical interface for inspecting and monitoring Kubernetes resources.

In this experiment, Minikube creates the local Kubernetes cluster, Kubernetes runs Nginx in a pod managed by a Deployment, and a NodePort service exposes the application. An environment variable is configured, and the dashboard is used to monitor the cluster.

---

## CONCLUSION

A local Kubernetes cluster was set up using Minikube and Hyper-V. An Nginx application was deployed, an environment variable was configured, and the application was exposed using a NodePort service. The application was accessed through a web browser, and the cluster resources were monitored using the Kubernetes dashboard.
